PASS BY VALUE:
1) This method copies the actual value of an argument into formal argument of the function.
2) In this case, changes made to the parameter inside the function have no effect on the actual argument.

PASS BY REFERENCE:
1) Method copies the address of an argument into the formal parameter.
2) Inside function, the address is used to acces the actual argument used in the call. This means that changes made to the parameter affects the argument.

POINTERS:
Pointer is a special variable used to store the address of an another variable to perform pass by reference.

eg: 
If we want to access the LED we cannot access it without its address.

WHY POINTERS?
1) To access the address of a variable.
2) To perform pass by reference.

Declaration of pointer:
datatype *ptr_name;
- *ptr_name, helps to store the address of the datatype.
* - helps to understand the level of the pointer
* - first-level pointer
* * - second - level pointer

- if i want to store the address of a variable:
eg:
int n = 10;
int *iptr = &n; 
output: 4
-stores the address of "n"
char ch = 'A';
char *cptr = &ch; 
output: 1
-stores the address of "ch"

-Address size and pointer size remains same for all datatypes and pointers.
- It is 4/8 bytes depends on the bitness of the systems.
- 32-bit system = 4 bytes
- 64-bit systems = 8 bytes
- most of our systems are 64-bit so size is 8 bytes.

RULES OF POINTERS:
1) Pointer is an integer
eg:
int n =10; //this integer is value
int *iptr = 5; //this integer is address, whatever is stored inside pointer is an address
- both looks like an integer, but can be differentiated by bus.(data bus, address bus)
- whatever we are putting inside databus - integer
- whatever we are putting inside address bus - pointer
eg:
int n = 10;
printf("%p\n, &n);
%p - to print the starting address of the variable

2) Referencing & Dereferencing
- Referencing - for getting the address(&)
- Dereferencing - going inside the address to see the value (*)
eg:
int n = 10;
int *iptr = &n; //&n - referencing
printf("%d\n, *iptr); // *ptr -dereferencing
 output: 10

- check notes for boxes.
- In printf "*" is used to get the value inside the address
- In int *iptr "*" is used to know the level of the pointer.
- when we gave *ptr, it goes to the address stored in pointer and return the value stored in that address.
- *ptr eg: *1000 = 10

eg: 
int n= 5;
int *ptr = &n;
*ptr = 10;
printf("%d", n);
output = 10
-here we are changing the value inside the address of n using dereferencing.

